NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":1,"id":"state-stack-mutation-round-3","children":[{"type":"text","value":"State stack mutation round 3"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Base revision: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"987f6ca0d"},{"type":"text","value":" ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"main"},{"type":"text","value":" after #2004 and #2008). The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/db"},{"type":"text","value":"\nsource was unchanged at the branch base."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"method","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Method"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Round 3 applied 47 plausible maintenance mistakes to the ported state stack:\n35 in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" and 12 in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sync.ts"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"lifecycle.ts"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"mutations.ts"},{"type":"text","value":".\nEach mutant ran against the full "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/db"},{"type":"text","value":" suite with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"--bail=1"},{"type":"text","value":". Of the\n47, 35 failed the suite and 12 survived. Each survivor received a probe that\npasses on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"main"},{"type":"text","value":", a code argument, or full-suite instrumentation that counts\nthe cases where the mutant changes a decision."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"survivors","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Survivors"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Mutant"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Change"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Verdict"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"S13"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Default "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rowUpdateMode"},{"type":"text","value":" becomes "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"full"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Gap. Closed here."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A sync delete that carries metadata keeps it"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Gap. Closed here."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A3"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An insert without metadata no longer clears metadata"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Gap. Closed here."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"S6"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"markReady"},{"type":"text","value":" runs before the truncate reapply"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Gap. Closed with a fix in #2033."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"S4"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The shared clear skips the pending direct-upsert marker"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Equivalent. The later retirement loop removes every confirmed key."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"L3"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The commit skips "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"restoreOrder"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Equivalent. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SortedMap"},{"type":"text","value":" restores order on the next ordered read or write."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"L4"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reappliedKeys"},{"type":"text","value":" starts as an empty set"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Equivalent. An empty set skips no key."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"X4"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A completed load-subset operation stays active"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Equivalent. Every reader ignores or tolerates a completed operation."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"M1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Previous row origins omit direct keys"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Equivalent on reached histories. Instrumentation found no differing virtual props."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"M2"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The commit copies the pre-sync visible state"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Equivalent by argument. The commit clears that state afterward."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"S10"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Previous virtual props ignore completed optimistic keys"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Equivalent on reached histories. See below."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"S3"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The commit keeps a cached enriched row"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Open. See below."}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A fifth mutant, RB1, came from this review. It makes\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rebuildAutomaticRowMetadataWrites"},{"type":"text","value":" overwrite explicit metadata writes. It\nsurvived the suite and the metadata publication oracle."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"gaps-closed-here","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Gaps closed here"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Partial row updates."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The optimistic-history and retention oracles always\nset "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rowUpdateMode: 'full'"},{"type":"text","value":". No test sent a partial update in the default mode.\nA separate partial lane now keeps the default mode. It reuses the generated\noptimistic histories and marks source batches partial from its own stream, so\nthe full-mode campaign's fixed seed 86103 still produces "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"main"},{"type":"text","value":"'s histories. A\npartial batch omits "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"c"},{"type":"text","value":" from its updates, and the model merges such an update\ninto its base row. The lane runs its own fixed (86104) and random campaigns.\nIts fixed campaign must write at least one partial update whose omitted "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"c"},{"type":"text","value":"\ndiffers from the source's held row. Two fixed histories merge a partial update\nwhile an accepted delete and an accepted schema-default re-insert overlay the\nrow. An in-suite wrong-answer witness writes the same rows in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"full"},{"type":"text","value":" mode and\nmust be rejected. S13 and MS, a mutant that merges onto the visible row\ninstead of the synced row, both fail the partial campaigns and those\nhistories."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Row metadata composition."}]},{"type":"text","value":" No test wrote a row delete that carried metadata,\nor an explicit set followed by an insert without metadata. The new\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"collection-row-metadata-composition-oracle.test.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" enumerates all 1,457\nlegal one-key histories of up to three writes, including "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"truncate"},{"type":"text","value":" and an\nidempotent re-insert, from three starting states. A last-write-wins model predicts the final metadata. Three\nlanes reach the immediate, held, and rebuilt production paths. The rebuilt lane\nalso varies the earlier held transaction over a key-2 update and every key-1\nrow write, for 1,767 histories. A1 and A3 fail all three lanes. RB1 fails only\nthe rebuilt lane. RI1, a mutant that computes metadata from a re-insert's\noriginal type, fails all three lanes. TR1, a mutant that keeps a\ntransaction's earlier metadata writes through a truncate, passes the rest of\nthe suite and fails all three lanes."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Maintainer decision."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The written contract covered only writes that carry\nmetadata. On 2026-10-05 the maintainer adopted the current behavior as the\ncontract for the rest: an insert without metadata clears the value, an update\nwithout metadata keeps it, a row delete or a truncate clears it, and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"metadata.row.set"},{"type":"text","value":" after a delete or a truncate keeps metadata for the absent\nrow. A second decision the same day made an insert equal to the held row an\nidempotent re-insert: without metadata it keeps the value."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"gap-closed-by-a-separate-fix","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Gap closed by a separate fix"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The ready-callback witness in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"collection-sync-reentrancy-oracle.test.ts"},{"type":"text","value":"\ncovered only an edit of a replaced key, through a subscriber with initial\nstate. Its sent-key filter hides a duplicate message. #2033 replaces it with a\nmodel-based grid and publishes ready-callback messages after the truncate\nbatch. Its own record covers the evidence."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"review-of-this-record","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Review of this record"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A code review of the first version found six items:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The insert-clears and update-keeps rules came only from production's helper.\nFixed: the maintainer decision above is now the authority."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Metadata kept for an absent row was pinned without a source. Fixed: the\nsame decision covers it."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The grammar had no "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"truncate"},{"type":"text","value":". Fixed: the grammar includes it, and TR1\nshows the gap."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The partial lane never ran with the schema default on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"c"},{"type":"text","value":". Fixed: four\nschema histories run it."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The reach counter counted writes, not writes that distinguish the modes.\nFixed: it counts only partial updates whose omitted "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"c"},{"type":"text","value":" differs from the\nheld row."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The record said that the counter fails under S13. The counter is computed\nby the driver and cannot fail under a production mutant. Fixed: the claim\nis removed."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"second-review-of-this-record","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Second review of this record"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A high-effort review found nine more items:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An insert equal to the held row keeps metadata, against the insert rule.\nFixed: the maintainer made it an idempotent re-insert, and the grammar\ncovers it."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The rebuilt lane's earlier transaction never touched key 1. Fixed: the lane\nvaries the earlier write. A mutant that keeps stale automatic writes (RB2)\nstill passes. In legal histories an open write keeps its type through the\nrebuild, so RB2 is equivalent there. A canceled earlier transaction would\nreach it, and this grammar does not generate one."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The schema-default histories could not distinguish a merge onto the\nsnapshot. Fixed: the new histories retain a default-carrying snapshot when\nthe partial update arrives, and MS fails them."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The partial field changed every history of the full-mode fixed seed.\nFixed: the partial lane has its own campaigns."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The partial lane had no in-suite wrong-answer witness. Fixed: the\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"partial-as-full"},{"type":"text","value":" witness."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sourceRows"},{"type":"text","value":" comment was wrong. Fixed."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"partialUpdates"},{"type":"text","value":" counter was unused. Removed."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"One "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"it"},{"type":"text","value":" ran all lifecycles under the default timeout. Fixed: each lane has\na timeout."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The immediate lane did not assert its path, and a throw lost its label.\nFixed."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"CodeRabbit then found that the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"partial-as-full"},{"type":"text","value":" witness was vacuous. The\ndriver's \"observation mutant reached its checkpoint\" assertion always failed\nfor it, and the witness accepted any assertion error, so it passed even with\nthe "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"full"},{"type":"text","value":" override removed. The configuration mutant is now exempt from that\nassertion, and the witness requires a row observation to reject it. With the\noverride removed, the witness fails."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"open-items","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Open items"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The follow-up record\n"},{"type":"link","href":"2026-10-05-round-3-followups.md","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"2026-10-05-round-3-followups.md"}]},{"type":"text","value":" resolves\nthe items this record left open. S3 was not equivalent in production builds,\nwhich the suite did not exercise, so the delete stays with new witnesses.\nPR #2030 removed S10's code on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"main"},{"type":"text","value":". The follow-up record also said that\n#2030 made RB2 unreachable. That was wrong: a transaction begun inside an open\none can commit first and reclassify the open one's inserts. See\n"},{"type":"link","href":"2026-10-06-nested-begin-metadata-rebuild.md","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"2026-10-06-nested-begin-metadata-rebuild.md"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"orc-outcomes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC outcomes"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This record repairs two grammars and adds one oracle, so ORC-012 applies."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-001: met."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Each oracle names its contract. The metadata contract is\nthe last-write-wins test in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"collection.test.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" plus the 2026-10-05\nmaintainer decision for writes without metadata. The partial-update contract\nis the default "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rowUpdateMode"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-002: met."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The optimistic-history model merges partial updates in its\nown base map. The metadata model folds writes over one value by the decided\nrules. Neither reads production state or imports its helper."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-003: met."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The new oracle's opening prose states the law and limits.\nProse beside the model, grammar, and driver explains each one. The\noptimistic-history prose now describes the partial-update lane."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-004: met."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The metadata grammar is exhaustive within its bound. A\ncontrol checks the count, named witnesses, and two excluded illegal\nhistories. The partial-update counter shows that the partial campaign writes\npartial updates whose omitted "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"c"},{"type":"text","value":" differs from the held row. The rebuilt\nlane's count control checks the earlier-write dimension."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-005: met."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Both oracles write through a real Collection's sync API and\nread public rows, change messages, and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"metadata.row.get"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-006: met."}]},{"type":"text","value":" S13, MS, A1, A3, RB1, TR1, and RI1 each fail the extended\noracles. The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"partial-as-full"},{"type":"text","value":" witness checks in the suite that the\npartial lane rejects a replacement. RB2 is equivalent within legal\nhistories. The metadata oracle also checks three named wrong\nanswers against its model."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-007: met for the generated property."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The partial lane runs a fixed\nseed (86104) and a random campaign under\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"collection-state.optimistic-history-partial"},{"type":"text","value":", which accepts a seed and path\nfor replay. The full-mode campaigns keep seed 86103 and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"main"},{"type":"text","value":"'s histories. The metadata\noracle enumerates its whole bounded domain, so it has no random campaign."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-008: met."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The optimistic-history model gains one flag. Two histories\nthat differ only in that flag can produce different base rows after a\npartial update, so the flag is needed."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-009: met."}]},{"type":"text","value":" \"Partial update\" and \"row update mode\" match the\nproduction option names."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-010: not applicable."}]},{"type":"text","value":" No shrinking or cleanup path changed. The new\noracle cleans up each Collection in a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"finally"},{"type":"text","value":" block."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-011: not applicable."}]},{"type":"text","value":" No reviewer named a fault shared by production\nand either model."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-012: met by this record."}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-013: met."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The metadata grammar includes neighbors on both sides of\neach rule: an update with and without metadata, and an insert before and\nafter an explicit set."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-014: not applicable."}]},{"type":"text","value":" No controlled provider or host supplies a\npremise."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"note-added-2026-10-05-settlement-drop","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note added 2026-10-05: settlement drop"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The settlement-drop change\n("},{"type":"link","href":"2026-10-03-settlement-drop.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"2026-10-03 review"}]},{"type":"text","value":") removes the retention\nof completed optimistic rows. Under that law, a delete and a re-insert can no\nlonger overlay the source row after they settle. The two fixed partial-update\nhistories therefore run the partial update while both transactions persist.\nThe update is held, and it publishes with the drop at settlement. The row keeps\nthe source's merged "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"c"},{"type":"text","value":". The partial-as-full witness is still rejected, now\nby the row observation at settlement. S13 still fails the retention oracle on\nthe merged revision, and S6 still fails the reentrancy oracle."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"state-stack-mutation-round-3","text":"State stack mutation round 3","level":1},{"id":"method","text":"Method","level":2},{"id":"survivors","text":"Survivors","level":2},{"id":"gaps-closed-here","text":"Gaps closed here","level":2},{"id":"gap-closed-by-a-separate-fix","text":"Gap closed by a separate fix","level":2},{"id":"review-of-this-record","text":"Review of this record","level":2},{"id":"second-review-of-this-record","text":"Second review of this record","level":2},{"id":"open-items","text":"Open items","level":2},{"id":"orc-outcomes","text":"ORC outcomes","level":2},{"id":"note-added-2026-10-05-settlement-drop","text":"Note added 2026-10-05: settlement drop","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"# State stack mutation round 3\n\nBase revision: `987f6ca0d` (`main` after #2004 and #2008). The `@tanstack/db`\nsource was unchanged at the branch base.\n\n## Method\n\nRound 3 applied 47 plausible maintenance mistakes to the ported state stack:\n35 in `state.ts` and 12 in `sync.ts`, `lifecycle.ts`, and `mutations.ts`.\nEach mutant ran against the full `@tanstack/db` suite with `--bail=1`. Of the\n47, 35 failed the suite and 12 survived. Each survivor received a probe that\npasses on `main`, a code argument, or full-suite instrumentation that counts\nthe cases where the mutant changes a decision.\n\n## Survivors\n\n| Mutant | Change | Verdict |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| S13 | Default `rowUpdateMode` becomes `full` | Gap. Closed here. |\n| A1 | A sync delete that carries metadata keeps it | Gap. Closed here. |\n| A3 | An insert without metadata no longer clears metadata | Gap. Closed here. |\n| S6 | `markReady` runs before the truncate reapply | Gap. Closed with a fix in #2033. |\n| S4 | The shared clear skips the pending direct-upsert marker | Equivalent. The later retirement loop removes every confirmed key. |\n| L3 | The commit skips `restoreOrder` | Equivalent. `SortedMap` restores order on the next ordered read or write. |\n| L4 | `reappliedKeys` starts as an empty set | Equivalent. An empty set skips no key. |\n| X4 | A completed load-subset operation stays active | Equivalent. Every reader ignores or tolerates a completed operation. |\n| M1 | Previous row origins omit direct keys | Equivalent on reached histories. Instrumentation found no differing virtual props. |\n| M2 | The commit copies the pre-sync visible state | Equivalent by argument. The commit clears that state afterward. |\n| S10 | Previous virtual props ignore completed optimistic keys | Equivalent on reached histories. See below. |\n| S3 | The commit keeps a cached enriched row | Open. See below. |\n\nA fifth mutant, RB1, came from this review. It makes\n`rebuildAutomaticRowMetadataWrites` overwrite explicit metadata writes. It\nsurvived the suite and the metadata publication oracle.\n\n## Gaps closed here\n\n**Partial row updates.** The optimistic-history and retention oracles always\nset `rowUpdateMode: 'full'`. No test sent a partial update in the default mode.\nA separate partial lane now keeps the default mode. It reuses the generated\noptimistic histories and marks source batches partial from its own stream, so\nthe full-mode campaign's fixed seed 86103 still produces `main`'s histories. A\npartial batch omits `c` from its updates, and the model merges such an update\ninto its base row. The lane runs its own fixed (86104) and random campaigns.\nIts fixed campaign must write at least one partial update whose omitted `c`\ndiffers from the source's held row. Two fixed histories merge a partial update\nwhile an accepted delete and an accepted schema-default re-insert overlay the\nrow. An in-suite wrong-answer witness writes the same rows in `full` mode and\nmust be rejected. S13 and MS, a mutant that merges onto the visible row\ninstead of the synced row, both fail the partial campaigns and those\nhistories.\n\n**Row metadata composition.** No test wrote a row delete that carried metadata,\nor an explicit set followed by an insert without metadata. The new\n`collection-row-metadata-composition-oracle.test.ts` enumerates all 1,457\nlegal one-key histories of up to three writes, including `truncate` and an\nidempotent re-insert, from three starting states. A last-write-wins model predicts the final metadata. Three\nlanes reach the immediate, held, and rebuilt production paths. The rebuilt lane\nalso varies the earlier held transaction over a key-2 update and every key-1\nrow write, for 1,767 histories. A1 and A3 fail all three lanes. RB1 fails only\nthe rebuilt lane. RI1, a mutant that computes metadata from a re-insert's\noriginal type, fails all three lanes. TR1, a mutant that keeps a\ntransaction's earlier metadata writes through a truncate, passes the rest of\nthe suite and fails all three lanes.\n\n**Maintainer decision.** The written contract covered only writes that carry\nmetadata. On 2026-10-05 the maintainer adopted the current behavior as the\ncontract for the rest: an insert without metadata clears the value, an update\nwithout metadata keeps it, a row delete or a truncate clears it, and\n`metadata.row.set` after a delete or a truncate keeps metadata for the absent\nrow. A second decision the same day made an insert equal to the held row an\nidempotent re-insert: without metadata it keeps the value.\n\n## Gap closed by a separate fix\n\nThe ready-callback witness in `collection-sync-reentrancy-oracle.test.ts`\ncovered only an edit of a replaced key, through a subscriber with initial\nstate. Its sent-key filter hides a duplicate message. #2033 replaces it with a\nmodel-based grid and publishes ready-callback messages after the truncate\nbatch. Its own record covers the evidence.\n\n## Review of this record\n\nA code review of the first version found six items:\n\n1. The insert-clears and update-keeps rules came only from production's helper.\n   Fixed: the maintainer decision above is now the authority.\n2. Metadata kept for an absent row was pinned without a source. Fixed: the\n   same decision covers it.\n3. The grammar had no `truncate`. Fixed: the grammar includes it, and TR1\n   shows the gap.\n4. The partial lane never ran with the schema default on `c`. Fixed: four\n   schema histories run it.\n5. The reach counter counted writes, not writes that distinguish the modes.\n   Fixed: it counts only partial updates whose omitted `c` differs from the\n   held row.\n6. The record said that the counter fails under S13. The counter is computed\n   by the driver and cannot fail under a production mutant. Fixed: the claim\n   is removed.\n\n## Second review of this record\n\nA high-effort review found nine more items:\n\n1. An insert equal to the held row keeps metadata, against the insert rule.\n   Fixed: the maintainer made it an idempotent re-insert, and the grammar\n   covers it.\n2. The rebuilt lane's earlier transaction never touched key 1. Fixed: the lane\n   varies the earlier write. A mutant that keeps stale automatic writes (RB2)\n   still passes. In legal histories an open write keeps its type through the\n   rebuild, so RB2 is equivalent there. A canceled earlier transaction would\n   reach it, and this grammar does not generate one.\n3. The schema-default histories could not distinguish a merge onto the\n   snapshot. Fixed: the new histories retain a default-carrying snapshot when\n   the partial update arrives, and MS fails them.\n4. The partial field changed every history of the full-mode fixed seed.\n   Fixed: the partial lane has its own campaigns.\n5. The partial lane had no in-suite wrong-answer witness. Fixed: the\n   `partial-as-full` witness.\n6. The `sourceRows` comment was wrong. Fixed.\n7. The `partialUpdates` counter was unused. Removed.\n8. One `it` ran all lifecycles under the default timeout. Fixed: each lane has\n   a timeout.\n9. The immediate lane did not assert its path, and a throw lost its label.\n   Fixed.\n\nCodeRabbit then found that the `partial-as-full` witness was vacuous. The\ndriver's \"observation mutant reached its checkpoint\" assertion always failed\nfor it, and the witness accepted any assertion error, so it passed even with\nthe `full` override removed. The configuration mutant is now exempt from that\nassertion, and the witness requires a row observation to reject it. With the\noverride removed, the witness fails.\n\n## Open items\n\nThe follow-up record\n[`2026-10-05-round-3-followups.md`](2026-10-05-round-3-followups.md) resolves\nthe items this record left open. S3 was not equivalent in production builds,\nwhich the suite did not exercise, so the delete stays with new witnesses.\nPR #2030 removed S10's code on `main`. The follow-up record also said that\n#2030 made RB2 unreachable. That was wrong: a transaction begun inside an open\none can commit first and reclassify the open one's inserts. See\n[`2026-10-06-nested-begin-metadata-rebuild.md`](2026-10-06-nested-begin-metadata-rebuild.md).\n\n## ORC outcomes\n\nThis record repairs two grammars and adds one oracle, so ORC-012 applies.\n\n- **ORC-001: met.** Each oracle names its contract. The metadata contract is\n  the last-write-wins test in `collection.test.ts` plus the 2026-10-05\n  maintainer decision for writes without metadata. The partial-update contract\n  is the default `rowUpdateMode`.\n- **ORC-002: met.** The optimistic-history model merges partial updates in its\n  own base map. The metadata model folds writes over one value by the decided\n  rules. Neither reads production state or imports its helper.\n- **ORC-003: met.** The new oracle's opening prose states the law and limits.\n  Prose beside the model, grammar, and driver explains each one. The\n  optimistic-history prose now describes the partial-update lane.\n- **ORC-004: met.** The metadata grammar is exhaustive within its bound. A\n  control checks the count, named witnesses, and two excluded illegal\n  histories. The partial-update counter shows that the partial campaign writes\n  partial updates whose omitted `c` differs from the held row. The rebuilt\n  lane's count control checks the earlier-write dimension.\n- **ORC-005: met.** Both oracles write through a real Collection's sync API and\n  read public rows, change messages, and `metadata.row.get`.\n- **ORC-006: met.** S13, MS, A1, A3, RB1, TR1, and RI1 each fail the extended\n  oracles. The `partial-as-full` witness checks in the suite that the\n  partial lane rejects a replacement. RB2 is equivalent within legal\n  histories. The metadata oracle also checks three named wrong\n  answers against its model.\n- **ORC-007: met for the generated property.** The partial lane runs a fixed\n  seed (86104) and a random campaign under\n  `collection-state.optimistic-history-partial`, which accepts a seed and path\n  for replay. The full-mode campaigns keep seed 86103 and `main`'s histories. The metadata\n  oracle enumerates its whole bounded domain, so it has no random campaign.\n- **ORC-008: met.** The optimistic-history model gains one flag. Two histories\n  that differ only in that flag can produce different base rows after a\n  partial update, so the flag is needed.\n- **ORC-009: met.** \"Partial update\" and \"row update mode\" match the\n  production option names.\n- **ORC-010: not applicable.** No shrinking or cleanup path changed. The new\n  oracle cleans up each Collection in a `finally` block.\n- **ORC-011: not applicable.** No reviewer named a fault shared by production\n  and either model.\n- **ORC-012: met by this record.**\n- **ORC-013: met.** The metadata grammar includes neighbors on both sides of\n  each rule: an update with and without metadata, and an insert before and\n  after an explicit set.\n- **ORC-014: not applicable.** No controlled provider or host supplies a\n  premise.\n\n## Note added 2026-10-05: settlement drop\n\nThe settlement-drop change\n([2026-10-03 review](2026-10-03-settlement-drop.md)) removes the retention\nof completed optimistic rows. Under that law, a delete and a re-insert can no\nlonger overlay the source row after they settle. The two fixed partial-update\nhistories therefore run the partial update while both transactions persist.\nThe update is held, and it publishes with the drop at settlement. The row keeps\nthe source's merged `c`. The partial-as-full witness is still rejected, now\nby the row observation at settlement. S13 still fails the retention oracle on\nthe merged revision, and S6 still fails the reentrancy oracle.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"# State stack mutation round 3\n\nBase revision: `987f6ca0d` (`main` after #2004 and #2008). The `@tanstack/db`\nsource was unchanged at the branch base.\n\n## Method\n\nRound 3 applied 47 plausible maintenance mistakes to the ported state stack:\n35 in `state.ts` and 12 in `sync.ts`, `lifecycle.ts`, and `mutations.ts`.\nEach mutant ran against the full `@tanstack/db` suite with `--bail=1`. Of the\n47, 35 failed the suite and 12 survived. Each survivor received a probe that\npasses on `main`, a code argument, or full-suite instrumentation that counts\nthe cases where the mutant changes a decision.\n\n## Survivors\n\n| Mutant | Change | Verdict |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| S13 | Default `rowUpdateMode` becomes `full` | Gap. Closed here. |\n| A1 | A sync delete that carries metadata keeps it | Gap. Closed here. |\n| A3 | An insert without metadata no longer clears metadata | Gap. Closed here. |\n| S6 | `markReady` runs before the truncate reapply | Gap. Closed with a fix in #2033. |\n| S4 | The shared clear skips the pending direct-upsert marker | Equivalent. The later retirement loop removes every confirmed key. |\n| L3 | The commit skips `restoreOrder` | Equivalent. `SortedMap` restores order on the next ordered read or write. |\n| L4 | `reappliedKeys` starts as an empty set | Equivalent. An empty set skips no key. |\n| X4 | A completed load-subset operation stays active | Equivalent. Every reader ignores or tolerates a completed operation. |\n| M1 | Previous row origins omit direct keys | Equivalent on reached histories. Instrumentation found no differing virtual props. |\n| M2 | The commit copies the pre-sync visible state | Equivalent by argument. The commit clears that state afterward. |\n| S10 | Previous virtual props ignore completed optimistic keys | Equivalent on reached histories. See below. |\n| S3 | The commit keeps a cached enriched row | Open. See below. |\n\nA fifth mutant, RB1, came from this review. It makes\n`rebuildAutomaticRowMetadataWrites` overwrite explicit metadata writes. It\nsurvived the suite and the metadata publication oracle.\n\n## Gaps closed here\n\n**Partial row updates.** The optimistic-history and retention oracles always\nset `rowUpdateMode: 'full'`. No test sent a partial update in the default mode.\nA separate partial lane now keeps the default mode. It reuses the generated\noptimistic histories and marks source batches partial from its own stream, so\nthe full-mode campaign's fixed seed 86103 still produces `main`'s histories. A\npartial batch omits `c` from its updates, and the model merges such an update\ninto its base row. The lane runs its own fixed (86104) and random campaigns.\nIts fixed campaign must write at least one partial update whose omitted `c`\ndiffers from the source's held row. Two fixed histories merge a partial update\nwhile an accepted delete and an accepted schema-default re-insert overlay the\nrow. An in-suite wrong-answer witness writes the same rows in `full` mode and\nmust be rejected. S13 and MS, a mutant that merges onto the visible row\ninstead of the synced row, both fail the partial campaigns and those\nhistories.\n\n**Row metadata composition.** No test wrote a row delete that carried metadata,\nor an explicit set followed by an insert without metadata. The new\n`collection-row-metadata-composition-oracle.test.ts` enumerates all 1,457\nlegal one-key histories of up to three writes, including `truncate` and an\nidempotent re-insert, from three starting states. A last-write-wins model predicts the final metadata. Three\nlanes reach the immediate, held, and rebuilt production paths. The rebuilt lane\nalso varies the earlier held transaction over a key-2 update and every key-1\nrow write, for 1,767 histories. A1 and A3 fail all three lanes. RB1 fails only\nthe rebuilt lane. RI1, a mutant that computes metadata from a re-insert's\noriginal type, fails all three lanes. TR1, a mutant that keeps a\ntransaction's earlier metadata writes through a truncate, passes the rest of\nthe suite and fails all three lanes.\n\n**Maintainer decision.** The written contract covered only writes that carry\nmetadata. On 2026-10-05 the maintainer adopted the current behavior as the\ncontract for the rest: an insert without metadata clears the value, an update\nwithout metadata keeps it, a row delete or a truncate clears it, and\n`metadata.row.set` after a delete or a truncate keeps metadata for the absent\nrow. A second decision the same day made an insert equal to the held row an\nidempotent re-insert: without metadata it keeps the value.\n\n## Gap closed by a separate fix\n\nThe ready-callback witness in `collection-sync-reentrancy-oracle.test.ts`\ncovered only an edit of a replaced key, through a subscriber with initial\nstate. Its sent-key filter hides a duplicate message. #2033 replaces it with a\nmodel-based grid and publishes ready-callback messages after the truncate\nbatch. Its own record covers the evidence.\n\n## Review of this record\n\nA code review of the first version found six items:\n\n1. The insert-clears and update-keeps rules came only from production's helper.\n   Fixed: the maintainer decision above is now the authority.\n2. Metadata kept for an absent row was pinned without a source. Fixed: the\n   same decision covers it.\n3. The grammar had no `truncate`. Fixed: the grammar includes it, and TR1\n   shows the gap.\n4. The partial lane never ran with the schema default on `c`. Fixed: four\n   schema histories run it.\n5. The reach counter counted writes, not writes that distinguish the modes.\n   Fixed: it counts only partial updates whose omitted `c` differs from the\n   held row.\n6. The record said that the counter fails under S13. The counter is computed\n   by the driver and cannot fail under a production mutant. Fixed: the claim\n   is removed.\n\n## Second review of this record\n\nA high-effort review found nine more items:\n\n1. An insert equal to the held row keeps metadata, against the insert rule.\n   Fixed: the maintainer made it an idempotent re-insert, and the grammar\n   covers it.\n2. The rebuilt lane's earlier transaction never touched key 1. Fixed: the lane\n   varies the earlier write. A mutant that keeps stale automatic writes (RB2)\n   still passes. In legal histories an open write keeps its type through the\n   rebuild, so RB2 is equivalent there. A canceled earlier transaction would\n   reach it, and this grammar does not generate one.\n3. The schema-default histories could not distinguish a merge onto the\n   snapshot. Fixed: the new histories retain a default-carrying snapshot when\n   the partial update arrives, and MS fails them.\n4. The partial field changed every history of the full-mode fixed seed.\n   Fixed: the partial lane has its own campaigns.\n5. The partial lane had no in-suite wrong-answer witness. Fixed: the\n   `partial-as-full` witness.\n6. The `sourceRows` comment was wrong. Fixed.\n7. The `partialUpdates` counter was unused. Removed.\n8. One `it` ran all lifecycles under the default timeout. Fixed: each lane has\n   a timeout.\n9. The immediate lane did not assert its path, and a throw lost its label.\n   Fixed.\n\nCodeRabbit then found that the `partial-as-full` witness was vacuous. The\ndriver's \"observation mutant reached its checkpoint\" assertion always failed\nfor it, and the witness accepted any assertion error, so it passed even with\nthe `full` override removed. The configuration mutant is now exempt from that\nassertion, and the witness requires a row observation to reject it. With the\noverride removed, the witness fails.\n\n## Open items\n\nThe follow-up record\n[`2026-10-05-round-3-followups.md`](2026-10-05-round-3-followups.md) resolves\nthe items this record left open. S3 was not equivalent in production builds,\nwhich the suite did not exercise, so the delete stays with new witnesses.\nPR #2030 removed S10's code on `main`. The follow-up record also said that\n#2030 made RB2 unreachable. That was wrong: a transaction begun inside an open\none can commit first and reclassify the open one's inserts. See\n[`2026-10-06-nested-begin-metadata-rebuild.md`](2026-10-06-nested-begin-metadata-rebuild.md).\n\n## ORC outcomes\n\nThis record repairs two grammars and adds one oracle, so ORC-012 applies.\n\n- **ORC-001: met.** Each oracle names its contract. The metadata contract is\n  the last-write-wins test in `collection.test.ts` plus the 2026-10-05\n  maintainer decision for writes without metadata. The partial-update contract\n  is the default `rowUpdateMode`.\n- **ORC-002: met.** The optimistic-history model merges partial updates in its\n  own base map. The metadata model folds writes over one value by the decided\n  rules. Neither reads production state or imports its helper.\n- **ORC-003: met.** The new oracle's opening prose states the law and limits.\n  Prose beside the model, grammar, and driver explains each one. The\n  optimistic-history prose now describes the partial-update lane.\n- **ORC-004: met.** The metadata grammar is exhaustive within its bound. A\n  control checks the count, named witnesses, and two excluded illegal\n  histories. The partial-update counter shows that the partial campaign writes\n  partial updates whose omitted `c` differs from the held row. The rebuilt\n  lane's count control checks the earlier-write dimension.\n- **ORC-005: met.** Both oracles write through a real Collection's sync API and\n  read public rows, change messages, and `metadata.row.get`.\n- **ORC-006: met.** S13, MS, A1, A3, RB1, TR1, and RI1 each fail the extended\n  oracles. The `partial-as-full` witness checks in the suite that the\n  partial lane rejects a replacement. RB2 is equivalent within legal\n  histories. The metadata oracle also checks three named wrong\n  answers against its model.\n- **ORC-007: met for the generated property.** The partial lane runs a fixed\n  seed (86104) and a random campaign under\n  `collection-state.optimistic-history-partial`, which accepts a seed and path\n  for replay. The full-mode campaigns keep seed 86103 and `main`'s histories. The metadata\n  oracle enumerates its whole bounded domain, so it has no random campaign.\n- **ORC-008: met.** The optimistic-history model gains one flag. Two histories\n  that differ only in that flag can produce different base rows after a\n  partial update, so the flag is needed.\n- **ORC-009: met.** \"Partial update\" and \"row update mode\" match the\n  production option names.\n- **ORC-010: not applicable.** No shrinking or cleanup path changed. The new\n  oracle cleans up each Collection in a `finally` block.\n- **ORC-011: not applicable.** No reviewer named a fault shared by production\n  and either model.\n- **ORC-012: met by this record.**\n- **ORC-013: met.** The metadata grammar includes neighbors on both sides of\n  each rule: an update with and without metadata, and an insert before and\n  after an explicit set.\n- **ORC-014: not applicable.** No controlled provider or host supplies a\n  premise.\n\n## Note added 2026-10-05: settlement drop\n\nThe settlement-drop change\n([2026-10-03 review](2026-10-03-settlement-drop.md)) removes the retention\nof completed optimistic rows. Under that law, a delete and a re-insert can no\nlonger overlay the source row after they settle. The two fixed partial-update\nhistories therefore run the partial update while both transactions persist.\nThe update is held, and it publishes with the drop at settlement. The row keeps\nthe source's merged `c`. The partial-as-full witness is still rejected, now\nby the row observation at settlement. S13 still fails the retention oracle on\nthe merged revision, and S6 still fails the reentrancy oracle.\n"}
